WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.424
Temporary directory selection is part of the security design.

2
00:00:03.424 --> 00:00:09.175
A per-application temporary directory can be easier to reason about than a
broad shared system location.

3
00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:13.439
In some systems, per-user, per-tenant, or per-job isolation is
appropriate.

4
00:00:13.439 --> 00:00:18.220
The right choice depends on deployment, sensitivity, runtime identity, and
workflow.

5
00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:26.168
What matters is that the location is intentional and that only appropriate
principals can read, write, modify, or delete the artifacts inside it.

6
00:00:26.168 --> 00:00:32.952
Permissions and ownership influence whether temporary files are
confidential and whether their integrity can be trusted.

7
00:00:32.952 --> 00:00:36.699
Directory permissions determine who can create or replace entries.

8
00:00:36.699 --> 00:00:39.930
File permissions determine who can read or modify content.

9
00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:43.161
Runtime identity determines which process owns the files.

10
00:00:43.161 --> 00:00:46.133
Umask and platform defaults may affect new file modes.

11
00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:53.434
Teams do not need to memorize every platform rule to apply the principle:
verify that permissions match the sensitivity and workflow.

12
00:00:53.434 --> 00:01:00.153
Containers and serverless platforms can make temporary storage feel
isolated, but assumptions should still be reviewed.

13
00:01:00.153 --> 00:01:05.193
Containers may have mounted volumes, shared nodes, writable layers, or
sidecar processes.

14
00:01:05.193 --> 00:01:12.365
Serverless runtimes may reuse execution environments or retain temporary
data longer than a single function call in some designs.

15
00:01:12.365 --> 00:01:19.602
Short-lived infrastructure is useful, but it is not a substitute for
sensitive-data minimization, controlled paths, and cleanup.

16
00:01:19.602 --> 00:01:23.607
Lifecycle management means temporary files exist only as long as needed.

17
00:01:23.607 --> 00:01:28.712
Cleanup should happen after success, failure, cancellation, timeout, and
exception paths.

18
00:01:28.712 --> 00:01:36.724
Deletion failures should be observable enough for operations to respond
before disk usage or sensitive artifact accumulation becomes a problem.

19
00:01:36.724 --> 00:01:41.634
Retention should be deliberate when temporary outputs must remain for
later processing.

20
00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:45.640
The absence of cleanup is a design decision, even when nobody intended it.
